Date: Sun, 21 Mar 93 11:21:57 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Pagan Trial

This is to let those of you hanging on your seat that "Da Judge" is still
deliberating in the challenge offered by Eric Raymond.

I had hoped to issue a verdict today, on the Vernal Equinox. Alas, I still
have reading to complete.

I found a copy of Margot Adler's "Drawing Down the Moon," recommended
highly in the FAQ and by others, and have been reading it and making notes.
I also skimmed Starhawk's "The Spiral Dance," some books by Z. Budapest, A.
Crowley, and others. And I even dug out a copy of Robert Graves' "The White
Goddess," which I'd once tried to read before in connection with my
interest in Thomas Pynchon's "Gravity's Rainbow."

Several self-described pagans on this list (gulp!) have sent me e-mail
explaining why they believe their religious practices are rational, for
which I'm grateful. And at two parties last night--one at Fen LaBalme's
place and one with several members of this list (Graps, Tribble, Sherwood,
Moulton, etc.) at the home of Forrest (ex of this list) and Rachel--I had a
chance to talk about paganism, witchcraft, objective reality vs.
metaphorical reality vs. feelgood reality, superstition, and modern
science. (I even talked to an Indian woman about flying yogis...she seemed
bemused that anyone would take the Vedas and Upanishads as anything other
than metaphor and myth.)

I take Mr. Raymond's challenge quite seriously, as you can see. I also see
it as a nice opportunity to take a break from my usual concerns (just got
Fudenberg and Tirole's "Game Theory," to help with some Mathematica
simulations) and to apply some principles of "Extropian thought" (dogma?)
to the question of why so many computer hacker types call themselves pagans
and witches.

You'll get my verdict as soon as I can get it decided and written.

-Tim May


