Neil Hammar responded to Tim May's post about avoiding government
control of information via offshore credit rating agencies by
writing:

    "No rational businessman in the US would even consider
    using such a database. . ."

Neil then went on to make a number of wild assertions as to what
would happen to a businessperson so foolish as to try to protect
himself from bad credit risks by using such a service.  I don't
know where Neil went to law school, but at the school I attended
we learned what the law really is and what it really can do.
When such a database is created, savvy businesspeople will use
it, and nobody will be able to do anything about it.

Even if an offshore credit agency couldn't advertise (it could,
internationally), word-of-mouth and "60 Minutes"-style exposes
would guarantee a steady stream of customers.  And nobody's
telephone bill would show anything if they used encryption,
anonymous remailers and stealth calling techniques (see my
upcoming "Norman French" article in MONDO 2000, #10).

I won't repeat, but heartily second, the comments make by Steve
Neal in his response to Neil Hammer's post.  The analogy between
such hypothetical offshore credit agencies and offshore banks is
dead bang right on.  Funny, the U.S. and all its lawyers are
virtually powerless against those cute little boutique banks in
those minuscule, nignog tax-and-banking havens.  I don't think
"Tim May's Caribbean Credit Barn" would have it much worse.

