Date: Sun, 21 Mar 93 22:13:58 GMT
From: "Stephen J. Whitrow" <sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk>
Subject: reason (semi-long)

> Some have been calling religions "irrational" because they
> contain false beliefs, so by that definition Newtonian physics
> is irrational, even if only slightly so.  It has _not_
> been demonstrated that religious beliefs are less useful, which I take
> to mean cause measurably more entropy, except by hearsay (eg "Mormons
> who don't stop at stop signs" -- in fact Mormons have above-average
> life expectancy).  I challenge all comers to come up with anti-entropic 
> benchmarks and use them to compare the entropy of rational vs. irrational 
> memes, just as a software engineer might compare the efficiency
> and functionality of computer programs.  Let's be rational about
> rationality.

> Nick Szabo					szabo@techbook.com

As we know, a belief might be irrational in the sense that its axioms are
false or it contains contradictions. Or to hold a belief might be irrational
since its consequences (physical or in terms of mental states) are entropic.
Or to use a complex model in certain applications could be irrational because
of the extra time taken. If the totally rational approach was to get as close
to reality as possible, then if I wanted to calculate pi x 2, I'd have to
spend the next five billion years calculating pi to a greater and greater
accuracy. This would be irrational. But let's try and sort memes by the
accuracy of their axioms and self-consistency, and see how they compare for
entropic / extropic outcomes.

My benchmark for anti-entropy is that individual human beings, currently the
most important entities in this sector of the galaxy, should be free to avoid
personal entropy if they so choose, and should always have the option of
self-transformation into a being far greater than a mere human. If a meme
promotes this then it passes my benchmark test. A meme that promotes personal
entropy is entropic. The only good entropic meme is a dead one. 

Taking the example of Newtonian physics, I think there's a vast difference
between its and religion's rationality. For over 200 years NP was the best
description of reality that we knew; its predictions were confirmed by 
observation. Even now in everyday situations excluding the microcosmic scale
or where velocity or gravitational field is very high, NP makes predictions
so close to General Relativity or QM that we can't observe any error. The
similarity between NP and GR in not-so non-Euclidean spacetime, for instance,
is related to the way the tan function approximates to a linear law for very
low angular values. Thus all the empirical evidence under these conditions
would show Newton to be true. 

For most of this century we have been aware that Newtonian physics is an
approximation, but to use it in an appropriate application is surely not
irrational. If I calculate 2 / 3 to four decimal places it doesn't mean
I believe 0.6667 is the true answer.

In my three determinants of the value of a belief -- Truth, Consequence and
Feelgood factor -- I'd probably start by assigning weightings in that
(descending) order. We're mainly concerned with the consequences here; 
although it may be worth looking at how the other factors compare.

Under Consequence we consider the physical personal entropic / extropic
effects of the belief on human beings (or whatever your species is at the
time). Do they gain or don't they? The Feelgood factor may be slightly raised
by the philosophical satisfaction of preferring one interpretation of the
evidence over another, or raised further by a meme adding to an individual's
overall sense of wellbeing provided by his / her thought patterns / schemata.
Or it could be the advantage of using a simpler model to save time. A
clinically accurate account of reality is 'emotionally neutral', so it has a
50% rating on this measure. If it had been adjusted to provide a more positive
view its Feelgood rating would be raised and its Truth lowered. In this case
the Consequence score would be raised if Truth was maintained within minimum
high standards and the optimism proved to be dynamic rather than passive.
Truth and Consequence would always be roughly in alignment; lower the Truth
rating below a minimum requirement and the Consequence would always plummet.
'Feelgood factor' would possibly always be out of phase with the other two,
at least until we are as demigods.
 
If an accurate belief motivated someone to act in an extropic manner, the
Consequence would rate highly, even though the belief did not contain
fantasies which instilled a short-term false sense of security or Feelgood.
Conversely, a false belief could destroy motivations to practise extropy,
providing a temporary Feelgood but with dire results.

- An example of a functional delusion: We all surely like to think that we are
more significant than we really are -- much more than specks in the cosmos. 
But this benefits all. We just have to try not to be too antagonistic!

- An example of how memes contribute to overall sense of wellbeing: Supposing
your world-view says that every 'good' has to be balanced out by an 'evil'.
The world is seen as being comprised of good and bad things, and let's say the
individual doesn't feel that life is that great. There doesn't seem to be
scope for improvement. Increasing good things is always nullified by increasing
bad things, and the hapless person seems trapped. But now, if we consider that
it's possible for a depressed person to see the world as terribly bleak and to
be full of negative thought patterns, we may reason that the opposite is
equally possible. Why not see the world in a positive light? Of course,
subjective mental states should not become too decoupled from objective
reality, so it's our job to try to improve the world. We have to try to
practise dynamic not passive optimism.

Extropianism passes my benchmark test with flying colours. It's based on the
real material world, seeks extropic outcomes, and encourages dynamic optimism!

What of the consequences of Newton's model? It provided the framework for
further advances in science and technology, and had itself been enabled by
previous descriptions of reality which were the most rational at the time. So
there was the Michelson-Morley experiment for instance, it wouldn't have been
performed until much later if everyone still thought we lived on the back of a
giant turtle, and spent two hours each day worshipping it. (Or making hourly
sacrifices to it.) So now science and technology has provided us with
computers, cars, digital watches, abundance of food, better health and longer
lifespans, easier lives, prospects for further gains etc.

However you view it, NP was either the best description of reality that was
available, or a very useful description for most applications that gives  
indistinguishable predictions from reality, or a very close approximation. It
scores very highly on the Truth measure of rationality.

Newtonian physics would get a rating something like this:

Truth           = 89 / 90
Consequence     = 6 / 6
Feelgood factor = 2 / 4
TOTAL SCORE     = 97%

But over the past 300 years, has Christianity for instance ever been a close
enough approximation to reality so that any rational person might believe in
it? The evidence of fossils was not recognised throughout all of this period.
Christianity claims that supernatural processes can occur; e.g. the sick can
be healed if someone with the 'power' takes their hand and says something
like, "I will; be thou clean." Or if you sell whatever you have and give to
the poor you will "have treasure in heaven". This last one is non-falsifiable,
but to base one's strategy on it is to take a terrible gamble. And how many
people have been cured of disease by 'faith-healing' compared to conventional
medicine? How many times have people starved because thousands of loaves and
fishes were not miraculously provided?

Let's say there is an omniscient, omnipotent, totally benevolent 'God'. But
'His' creations have experienced suffering (for nearly 6,000 years at least).
Therefore 'He' is either a mere non-omnipotent 'he/she/it', or is both
omnipotent and malicious. All the evidence fails to support the theory, and
it is not internally self-consistent. On a Truth measure of rationality, it
scores either very low, or zero.

We're living in a physical universe -- the laws of physics place constraints
on what is possible. There is no scientific evidence that 'supernatural'
events occur, and since by definition they contravene natural law, such
beliefs are irrational.

What benefits are provided? Poverty, death, disease, human sacrifice,
suffering, etc, are great, according to religions. Humans should not place
themselves first -- they only exist to serve the Creator. Still, the benefits
are supposed to be 'spiritual' not material. But for any given individual,
there ought to be a complete set of beliefs, thought patterns, values etc that
are fairly close to the truth, and specifically suited to optimising that
individual's enjoyment of life and motivating him / her into following an
extropic strategy. Rather than pick some religion and swallow it hook line and
sinker, it's better for individuals to construct their own personal ideology,
picking elements that they like from anywhere and rejecting what they see as
harmful. Someday there should be powerful programs where you enter in some of
your most important beliefs and values -- the machine then suggests changes
and you continue negotiating with it until acceptable changes are found....
 
Religion lulls its believers into a false sense of security: God / Krishma /
spirits / stars or whatever will look after you and protect you, there is far,
far more to the world than is apparent, so you shouldn't strive to save
yourself from personal extinction since it is not really what it appears......
Some practitioners fleece the gullible out of fortunes, but the real tragedy
of the commons is the forced genocide that results for participants and
non-participants alike.

If 100 years ago people had rejected beliefs about the 'soul' surviving ten
years' burial etc, and developed memes that greatly enhanced the value of human
life, the resulting extra incentive to defeat death would have diverted efforts
towards achieving this goal, instead of fighting wars to show which state has
the most firepower, and making apologies for meekly facing the axeman. There
is an epidemic of memes such as statism, supernaturalism, mortalism, radical
altruism etc, as a million humans perish every week, a hundred per minute. We
ought to be asking what replacement memeset over the past 100 years would have
catalysed genuine progress over that time, such that mature nanotechnology
might even now be readily available to all who wished to take advantage of it. 

On my scale, Christianity would register like this:

Truth           = 3 / 90
Consequences    = 0 / 6
Feelgood factor = 3 / 4

Christians would assign a much higher importance to the Feelgood factor; if
empirical evidence suggested that their belief was false they would concoct
some mystical explanation for this involving 'God's will'. On my scale, 
85 / 90 would be a minimum standard for Truth; those with a great capacity 
for faith would accept much lower standards. 

>From what I've seen of neopaganism, it would in any case score higher than
Christianity. Stripped of its irrational / supernaturalistic / (I'd call it
"mystical") excesses, it might nearly rate with Newtonian physics. To qualify
as being 'rational', it would need to have extropic consequences for its
participants, and they would have to recognise that explanations for any
experiences were rational rather than supernaturalistic. But it doesn't matter
if the details of these explanations haven't yet been worked out by science.
My dictionary describes pagans as 'heathens', who are described as 'pagans' or
as _not_ adhering to a religious system. Under this description, I'm a pagan.

What about Marxism? This would score very close to zero as far as almost
everyone is concerned. For the tiny minority, powercrats who cynically 
exploit it to benefit themselves, we'd have something like 1 / 90 (they
don't believe in it as they believe they should be more equal than others),
6 / 6 (at least, until someone gets them with an ice-pick or something),
4 / 4 (well, they think it's good for them). In this example, vodka-swilling
caviar-stuffing limousine-riding dacha/palace-owning classes would only assign
'Truth' a very low weighting. For the proletariat, Consequence scores 0 / 6.

I agree that there are efficient functional delusions; the memepool is
enriched by including the cutting edge of arch-empiricism to enable us to
physically manipulate the world, and extropic functional delusions which
emphasise or downgrade parts of reality to help with our motivations. However,
if one person was allowed to subsist on wholly rational beliefs and another
was forced to reject all reason, I'd bet on the rational person to win.

I certainly wouldn't like to see extropianism linked to 'religion' -- if we
tried to develop an additional movement to aid the promotion of extropy it
should satisfy a certain minimum standard of objectivity. But I don't think
the arch-empirical, reductionist, emotionally-neutral approach is the best for
all applications.

