philosophy, not an empirical benchmark of anti-entropy.  I'm not 
going to quarrel with the philosophy, other than to note that it 
has failed, as have all other philosophical systems, to demonstrate 
empirically that it is anti-entropic.

Ideally, an empirical benchmark is an actual physical measure of
anti-entropy or organizational complexity, based on information
theory and thermodynamics.  Meanwhile, here are some other measures
that relate to anti-entropy, based on theoretical models of
anti-entropy which I will elaborate on if desired:

benchmark		theoretical basis
---------		-----------------
memetic growth rate	memetic extropy (short-term memetic extropy)
fertility		memetic host extropy (long-term memetic extropy,
			intrinsic boundless expansion)
life expectancy		individual host extropy	
education level, years	Kolmogorov complexity

For life expectency the best numbers we have now are the standard
life insurance tables; we need to add a life expectancy futures market 
to evaluate the impact of cryonics, etc.  The education level I consider 
a quite weak benchmark, but we need some widely measured quantity to
reflect the Kolmogorov complexity of a meme, to compliment 
measures of memetic growth.

Evaluating various memes based on these benchmarks is left as an
exercise for the student.  I welcome further suggestions for empirical 
benchmarks, and analysis relating these and the above benchmarks to 
measurable physical entropy.



