From: David Cake <davidc@cs.uwa.edu.au>
Subject: MEDIA: Manufacturing Consent 

>From my libertarian but only partially extropian point of
> view, Chomsky is worth disregarding. It's not so much that his
> facts are wrong (though occasionally they are) as that they
> are grossly out of context. He is filled with the delusion
> that competing factions cannot all be wrong at the same time--
> that the US's past and present behavior (bad as it has often
> been) is not entirely sufficient excuse for bad behavior by
> Khmer Rouge, Sandinistas, and other Soviet stooges.

> To hear Chomsky tell it, nobody would ever torture or oppress
> their subjects were they not driven to it by the US.

> In fact, now that I think about it, Chomsky makes a very good
> example of entropic thinking: everything happens at the cost
> of something else, there's no room for expansion.

I think that you have completely missed Chomsky's message entirely. You
appear to have responded so strongly to minor parts of the film that
you have missed the central message. Or perhaps you have missed the film,
but responded to the enquiry on the basis of Chomsky's beliefs overall
(in which case you are a good example of biased and blinkered thinking -
I do not agree with some of this mans politics, so I will ignore all he
says on any issue - almost certainly a bigger crime than entropic 
thinking in general).
	I also think that you are wrong in your assessment of Chomskys
views on the US - he is quite pro-US in general. He points our several
times that activism of the kind he participates in is only necessary and
possible because the US is a democracy with a great degree of freedom for
its inhabitants (comparitively), and that he directs most of his protests
against actions of the US government because he is a US citizen. The US
media is already reporting quite well on the atrocities of others, so
he feels it his duty to inform as to the misdeeds of the US. A simplified
version of his feelings, but I repeat the basis that Chomsky is very
pro-US because it allows him as a citizen to criticize it, and in
admitting that that right is a good thing, you are admitting that for
people to criticize the US is a good thing. 
	And now my own review of the film - the film is one that I found
thought provoking and interesting, despite its length (about 3 hrs). The
film concentrates not on Chomskys views of US government policy, but on
his views of media coverage in the US, and his views on free speech.
I found his criticisms of the media to be worthwhile - there are
criticisms that can be made of his view, but none that entirely condemn
it. His view is very roughly that big media is inherently conservative,
not because of the vested interests of the owners in big business, or
because of government control, but because a relatively radical point of
view is not what most people want to hear, so big media must criticise 
only from a fairly conservative position in order to achieve maximum
circulation. If you have serious criticism of this view, please either
see the film or read the book, and then criticise it, because I am both
less articulate and have less time to talk about it than Chomsky. He does
not condemn the media as particularly wrong- he does not see the answer
as changing big media organisations, imposing controls as to their views.
He sees the answer as making as many people as possible aware of the
medias inherent bias, and then advising them to seek as many other
sources of information as possible. I do not think that this is a view
that libertarians/extropians are likely to have major arguments with,
though the directions in which they feel big media is too conservative
are likely to be different ones than Chomsky (he dislikes how they
portray third world politics, extropians dislike the way they portray
cryonics or the FDA). The example of media coverage is Cambodia vs. the
Indonesia in East Timor, which I guess many people on the list will 
disagree with Chomskys views on, but still serves as a good comparison 
of media coverage of too relatively similar situations.
	The film also talks about Chomskys views on free speech. He is
very pro free speech, to the extent that he is willing to defend Nazi
historians right to state their views, etc. It also talks somewhat about
his life.
	I was intending to ask people about their views on Chomsky. He
is an anarcho-syndicalist, so not too far politically different, but I
have noticed that libertarianism tends to be so anti-socialist that it
often ends up very pro-US, so I guessed he might not be popular just
because of his anti-US stance on many world politics questions. I
actually think that Chomskys political position in the abstract is one
that I generally feel fairly happy with. He generally only appears on
the media when they want someone to give an anti-US view, so people
unfamiliar with him probably think that is the extent of his views. I
feel that he is a worthwhile political thinker, though not one I expect
the libertarians on the list to agree with completely.

